<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="forms-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="forms-title">Old forms and new forms</h2>
      <p>The law that applies generally follows when the amount was credited or paid, whichever was earlier, and not when you file. Secondary sources report a 31 March 2026 cut-off, so January to March 2026 stays on the old forms even when filed later.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Form mapping, scrolls sideways on small screens">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Used for</th><th scope="col">Up to 31 March 2026</th><th scope="col">From 1 April 2026, as reported</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Quarterly TDS on salary</th><td>Form 24Q</td><td>Form 138</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Quarterly TDS on other payments to residents</th><td>Form 26Q</td><td>Form 140</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Quarterly TDS on payments to non-residents</th><td>Form 27Q</td><td>Form 144</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Quarterly TCS</th><td>Form 27EQ</td><td>Form 143</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Salary TDS certificate</th><td>Form 16</td><td>Form 130</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Non-salary TDS certificate</th><td>Form 16A</td><td>Form 131</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Annual tax statement</th><td>Form 26AS</td><td>Form 168</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
    <p class="section-foot">Sources agree on 138 and 140. Other rows come from fewer sources, and one source said the rules and portal formats were still being finalised in mid-2026. Confirm against the current form on the official portal. The same sources give quarterly return dates of 31 July, 31 October, 31 January and 31 May, and monthly deposit by the 7th, with March deductions by 30 April. Check before relying on them.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="cycle-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="cycle-title">The cycle behind each return</h2>
      <p>The return is the visible part. Most problems begin earlier.</p>
    </header>
    <ol class="cycle">
      <li><h3>Identify</h3><p>Decide whether TDS applies to a payment, at what rate, and under which provision.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Deduct</h3><p>Deduct at the correct time, using the payee's correct PAN.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Deposit</h3><p>Pay to the government against the right section and tax year.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Report</h3><p>File the quarterly return with deduction and challan details.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Certify</h3><p>Issue certificates to payees.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Correct</h3><p>Check defaults and file corrections if needed.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="wrong-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="wrong-title" class="first-h2">Why TDS credit goes missing</h2>
      <p>When a vendor or employee says "my TDS isn't showing", one of these is the usual reason:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Wrong or invalid PAN</strong> in the return, so credit goes elsewhere or not at all.</li>
        <li><strong>Return not filed or filed late,</strong> though tax was deducted.</li>
        <li><strong>Challan details that do not match,</strong> including wrong section or year.</li>
        <li><strong>Transition errors:</strong> new form numbers used for transactions before April 2026, old section codes on new-year returns, or an assessment year chosen on a challan instead of a tax year.</li>
        <li><strong>Timing.</strong> The deductor's filing may simply not have reached the statement yet.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="consequence">If it is late or wrong</h2>
      <p>Reported consequences include late-filing fees, interest on late deposit and possible penalties. Section numbers differ by period and sources vary in how they cite them, so ask a professional about your case, especially if you have older quarters pending.</p>

      <h2 id="split">What stays with you</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Give payment details and correct PANs on time.</li>
        <li>Make or approve the deposit. Filing someone's return does not pay the tax.</li>
        <li>Keep control of your TAN login and registered contacts.</li>
        <li>Check that certificates go out, and keep copies.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="ask">Questions to ask a provider</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>How do you handle the two regimes for this year's transition?</li>
        <li>Who checks TDS applicability, you or me?</li>
        <li>Will you check the default statements and handle corrections?</li>
        <li>Are certificates included?</li>
        <li>Is correcting past quarters charged separately?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="fees">What affects the fee</h2>
      <p>Number of deductees and challans, salary and non-salary returns, whether corrections are included, and past defaults to clean up. Ask for the fee in writing for a defined scope.</p>

      <h2 id="overlap">Salaries?</h2>
      <p>Salary TDS sits within the wider monthly work of {% routeLink "/services/payroll-services/", "payroll" %}, including attendance, payslips and, where they apply, PF and ESI.</p>

